And they, the leader took out his rubber truncheon and hit me on the head. And I was lying in the gutter. And he ground his heels into my glasses. And the people passed by and nobody came to my assistance.

INT: What year was this?

BH: [SIGHS] In 1927, it was six years before Hitler came to power. So antisemitism was always there. It wasn't as virulent as it became later, when it was organized by the state.

INT: Was there antisemitism at the college?

BH: At the college?

INT: Yes.

BH: No. At least, I didn't experience it.

INT: What happened after you finished the college? Where did you go?

BH: No, I went to Montreal. Actually, what happened, my Hamburg firm wanted to transfer me to their New York office. And you could not get into the United States under German passport. There was, since World War I, anti-German quota.

So I went to Canada first, to Montreal. And I worked in the Gramophone Record Company, which belonged to a-- a friend of my father's.

INT: Did you speak English?

BH: Yes. What I had learned at a technical high school. And I went to McGill University in Montreal, in the evenings, to improve my English. But I-- I couldn't speak any German, anymore. So I-- I became bilingual very quickly. And--

INT: When did you return to Germany?

BH: In '31, from New York City. Because when the big economic depression set in, after the stock market crash in '29, wages were cut. I used to earn $25 a week. And one could live on that. And it was cut to $20. And I said no, I can't live on that.

So I went back. I expected-- my father I had a business-- that ultimately, I would be taken into it. That was always the idea.

INT: Had life changed in Germany, when you came back?

BH: Yes.

INT: What way?

BH: Well, the Hitler movement was growing tremendously. And the antisemitic propaganda was most unpleasant. And I said to my father 'Right away, settle up. Get out. This can't do any good.'

He said, 'What makes you think so? I served in the German army. I got the Iron Cross. What can happen to me?'

I said, just read the paper. Now, the famous Nazi paper was the VolkischerBeobachter.The-- and Goebbels, who was propaganda [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] minister later, he wrote an article. 'We shall round up all the Jews in Europe and create a vast Jewish city in Lublin, in Poland.' Lublin became Treblinka, later, of evil fame.

So I said to my father this statement of Goebbels-- he was in not yet minister, it was before he came to power-- it presupposes that the Germans will conquer all Europe. Because up to now, they're only fighting the Jewish community of 1/2 a million out of 65 million Germans. But what does it mean, all the Jews of Holland, of Belgium, of France, of Italy? It-- they mean to conquer Europe.

He said, 'Don't be a fool. Do you believe what they write?' I said, 'Yes, I do.' I read MeinKampf,which was the official Bible of Hitler, had written whilst in prison. After his uprising in Munich, in '23, he was imprisoned for a few years. So I said it's time to get out whilst the going is possible. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Now, once on the 13th of January, '33, Hitler came to power, we all expected pogrom that night. And my father dispersed his family, except me. And he said, here is my army rifle and my revolver. If they break in, you got my permission to shoot down the intruders.

So he and-- he and I remained behind the shutters. We let down the shutters. And now, naturally, the marauding Stormtroopers and Brownshirts came through our street. And they went into various Jewish flats-- not into our house. And they passed our house. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

How my father could have expected me, a 23-year-old son, to shoot down Nazis? Well, can you imagine? I mean, I wouldn't be sitting here, what they would have done in revenge, you know?

But I stood behind them. Oh, I was very proud. I carried a rifle. I could shoot.

INT: You were talking about anti-Jewish propaganda. What sort of propaganda was there?

BH: Well, give an instance. On the 1st of April, '33, there was a general boycott day. All-- every Jewish shop was boycotted. That means storm troopers stood with cross drivers in front of the door and wouldn't let customers in.

Now, I was traveling for my father's business. I was in Westphalia, in a town called Herford, H-E-R-F-O-R-D. And there was a retailer merchant, by the name of [NON-ENGLISH]. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And I wanted to get in. I didn't know he was Jewish. There were two Brownshirts, said, 'You can't go into the shop. The owner is a Jew.'

So I said to him, 'So am I.' He said, 'Makes no difference. You're not allowed to come in.'

So I went back to the railway station, went back to Hannover. I said to my father, 'I'm not going, traveling anymore in Germany.' He said 'Why?'

I told him what had happened. He said, 'Well, that's an isolated case.' But I said, 'How can you say it's isolated? It's general boycott day, throughout the country.'

Anyway, I refused to travel anymore. And he said, well I must call on customers in Hannover. And there's one particular incident, so I entered the shop. Apparently, no traveler had been there for many years.

And the man greeted me quite nicely. He said I've done business with your grandfather. He was a very nice old gentleman.

So we were talking. And he complained about the slackness in the retail trade. So I said, you know, I met the chairman of your organization, a man called [? Jacobs ?] who lives in Goslar, G-O-S-L-A-R, in the Harz Mountains.

He was elected chairman last year. He said 'Yes. You see, he was elected chairman.' 'But he is a Jew. He's got no right to be chairman of our organization.'

I said, 'But who elected him? There are hardly any retail shops in-- owned by Jews. It's your Christian friends who elected him.' He said, 'It makes no difference. A Jew must not put himself forward and become so prominent.' [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

I went back to-- to my father. And I said, you see? What's the good of staying here? Sell up whilst you can.

Because it-- in the first year of Hitler coming to power, each adult child could take 30,000 marks out of the country. Right? So my father waited till he was sent out after, to Cuba, with 10 marks in his pocket.

INT: Can I take you back a little bit? We've just talked about the time that Hitler was in power. What-- what-- what do you remember about a time leading up to him coming into power?

BH: Well, I remember the-- the fighting in the streets, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] between the various ex-service organizations. They--

INT: We'll take a tape break here.

BH: Mhm.

INT: This is tape three. I'm interviewing Bernhard Herzberg. We just touched on the time leading up to Hitler coming into power, and you were telling me you remember the fighting in the streets. Was that rich-- who was fighting whom in those days?

BH: When I came back from the United States in 1931, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] mass unemployment had risen to almost 6 million people. The ex-servicemen were organized along political lines. The conservative-- the German nationalist, as distinct from the Nazis-- formed the [? Stahlhammer, ?] which means the steel hammered. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The Nazis, of course, had their own brown-- brown shirts and black shirts. The social democrats and trade unionists had the Reichsbanner, it means the flag of the nation, which was black, red, gold, which is, again, now the flag of the new Germany.

And then the communists had the Red Front Fighters' organization. Now, all these various groups used to fight each other at [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] meeting halls, or in the streets.

In fact, the famous song of the Nazis, the 'Horst-Wessel-Lied,' was composed by a chap called [? Schlagater, ?] who was shot by the French during the occupation of the Rhineland in '23. He was actually a pimp. But the Nazis elevated him to a hero because he composed this famous song and the text. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

Especially the communists and the Nazis used to fight each other. And I've been to meetings in Hamburg, for instance, where the Nazis entered the meeting hall, organized by the social democrats, and used to wrench the legs off wooden chairs and just beat up anybody in the hall, or used beer mugs. It was very violent.

And of course, already in 1922, the Jewish foreign minister, Walther Rathenau, was assassinated in the streets. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] They also murdered Erzberger, who was a Catholic, because he signed the peace treaty of-- at Versailles in 1919 .

So-- and there's a strange thing happened, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] according to this Treaty of Versailles, Germany was limited to 100,000 military-- men in uniform. So they made an arrangement with Soviet Russia, and they trained the Black Reichswehr, the secret German army in the Soviet Union.

So this collaboration between Nazis and the communists, which afterwards, materialized in 1939 with the Nazi Soviet non-aggression pact, well, that had its basis in the secret training. The communists said, we are also against the Treaty of Versailles. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Because Lenin said no annexations, no reparations, and all that.

But the main reason for the Hitler movement coming to fruition was the great inflation of 1923, when one United States dollar was valued at 4,2 billion Reichsmark. So that meant that the life savings of every person, especially the middle class, was wiped out overnight.

This popularization of the middle class to the level of the proletariat, that was the main reason for the rise of the Hitler movement. They said, it's-- the Jews caused it. Of course, no proof was given. I mean, the Jews were 500,000 out of 65 million, less than 5% of the population, but the usual scapegoats.

INT: So once Hitler had come to power, how did that start affecting the life of the Jews? You talked about a boycott of the shops.

BH: Not only that.

INT: What else happened?

BH: My brother-in-law, my sister's husband was an attorney. The Nazis would-- only men who served in World War I in the German army, and were still in the legal profession, they could stay. All the others were immediately kicked out. Every teacher, every judge, every civil servant, every doctor lost his job overnight.

Businesses were boycotted. I give examples of what I encountered on a small scale. It was so obvious to me-- I urged my father, please get out. Boy, you're too young fool. There's always been antisemitism in Germany. We were only emancipated in 1823 through the Code Napoleon. The French emperor passed a decree which the Prussian king had to accept.

So I said to him, if you knew there was always antisemitism, you shouldn't have begotten me, and make me be subjected to this kind of education. He said, how can you now say I-- I shouldn't have had kids? And I said, no, Jews shouldn't have children and afterwards expose them to this kind of fate.

INT: What was the relationship between you and your father like in those days?

BH: Actually, quite cordial, as long as I got first-class results at school, or high school, or technical school, or maintained myself for three years in Canada and the United States on the minimum subsistence level, I was OK. But [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] he was of the opinion, whatever he gave to his family was charity. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The first Saturday after my return, he said, you must come to synagogue with me. I said, why? It's customary that the Prodigal Son [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] must worship on the first Sabbath. So I went along.

And I was called up to the bima and had to say a blessing. And then, I was asked to donate-- make a donation. And I said, I'm sorry, I haven't got a penny. I came back from America because of the economic depression. And when you earn $20 a week, you can't save a penny.

So my father, to my great embarrassment, said to the beadle, you see here my son, whom I sent to America to make a fortune. He comes back as poor as a church mouse. He's really a schlemiel.

You know what the word schlemiel is? You know that in English-- incompetent person. And I was absolutely livid. That's what-- that gives you an idea why he afterwards kicked me out of the house.

INT: What happened then?

BH: In May '33, my twin sister tried to commit suicide. And my elder sister pulled the gas tube out of her mouth and rescued her. And my father, when I came home at night from work, asked me to come into his study, and he told me that my twin sister nearly succumbed, but was rescued in the last moment.

And he was livid, absolutely livid. In our circles, you don't do that. I said, what do you mean in our social circle one doesn't commit suicide? Where is it laid down?

She must be isolated. She must not be visited by a single member of the family. I refuse to tell you where she is.

So I gave him blazes. Said, how can you be so cruel? You appear to be so charitable. You are in charge of the Hebrew congregation, you bury the dead. And here's your own child-- you-- one should rush to her and help her. He refused. And I said, I would make it known throughout the community the way you behave towards your child. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The next evening, when I came home, I found the key to the front door didn't work. So I rang the bell. The servant girl came and said, your father had the lock changed so that you should never cross the threshold again. Here is the receipt from the Hannover railway station. Your mother packed all of your belongings into two suitcases. You can pick up your things, and slammed the door in my face.

Then she opened again. Oh, I forgot to mention, you're dismissed without notice.

INT: What did you do?

BH: I had a few pennies in my pocket. And I remember walking into that street, and every house had a swastika flag, or imperial flag-- the black, white, and red of-- of imperial Germany hanging.

And thereby, also hangs a story. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] The Nazi government said, to greet the new government, every house had to show its loyalty to the new government by hanging out the flag. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] My father couldn't buy the swastika flag, which was a Nazi emblem, so he bought the old imperial flag, black, white, and red, the symbol of the nationalists, who were also antisemitic, but he thought it would protect him from Nazi marauders.

I had taken that flag down and burned it in the cellar in the boiler room. Of course, there was a-- that was prior to my being ejected.

I only went back to that house once when my grandmother came to say goodbye to me, and 30 years later, when I visited Hannover, then the-- it had become [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] an animal research station. There were 25 rooms in that villa.

Before we do the next tape, I'd like to show your pictures. It's quite interesting. Still-- that building still stands. For some reason, it escaped bombing.

INT: What I forgot to ask you, when we were talking about Jews losing their professions, what about your father's business? Could he hang on to that?

BH: Well, he carried on. And when I left the country, he was still in business. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I-- I believe his Aryan secretary took over afterwards. The business were Aryanized, as they called it, you know.

And the editor of the Nazi newspaper walked into the villa, and said to my parents, get out. I've taken over. That's how it was done, just confiscation without any--

INT: What-- what year was that?

BH: I don't know. I wasn't there anymore. I think it must have been '36 or so.

INT: So you had been thrown out of your parental home. Where did you go?

BH: I borrowed 150 marks from a friend and went to Holland-- I crossed the border at Krefeld to get into a town called Nijmegen-- in Germany we called it Nimwegen.

INT: When was this?

BH: In '33.

INT: When in '33?

BH: I think in June.

INT: And--

BH: The Dutch police arrested me at the station and said, we have enough unemployed. When they asked me, what do you want to do in Holland? I said, looking for work.

I was given two weeks grace, because I-- a girlfriend of mine was living with her Dutch uncle in Nijmegen. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I went over the-- they pushed me over the Belgian border.

And in Brussels, there was a leather fair. And I said was trained in tanning and in leather, generally, I went there by chance, the fair took place. And I met a tanner from Tirlemont in Belgium, Mr. [? Kemp. ?] And I'll never forget him. And he happened to be Jewish.

And he said-- I begged him, give me a job. He said, you're a fool. I said, why? Why do you stay in Europe?

It's clear to me-- this was in June '33, yeah, the Nazis will overrun Holland and Belgium, break neutrality. They will conquer France in a couple of weeks. And you're looking for work in Europe? Every country in Europe will be occupied by the Nazis, bar England, because they haven't crossed the Channel yet.

France were-- that's nothing. The Maginot Line, that was that line of fortifications. And they will go behind it through Belgium. He said, get out of Europe.

INT: Can I just take you back a bit? The day you left Germany, could you describe that day?

BH: Yes, well October-- you mean, the first time, or for South Africa?

INT: Well, when you left for Holland, Belgium, and then eventually, South Africa.

BH: No, I went on my own.

INT: How about your family? Did you say goodbye to your family?

BH: They didn't know. I wasn't staying at home. You see, I found out after my dismissal from the business that it was unlawful, because I had given him no reason in business. He was annoyed with me because I criticized him over his behavior, in relation to my twin.

So I went to the Labor Department, and I said, I'm claiming the dole-- the-- I told the official the story. Said, don't be ridiculous, your father's a rich man. Besides, in Germany, in the distributive trade, you could only be dismissed six weeks before the end of a quarter of a year.

So [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I sent a letter to my father, you got to pay me out for a quarter of a year.

INT: Did you say goodbye to your mother?

BH: My mother condoned it. I could never understand why. Except, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] she was subjected to his behavior.

I mean, he criticized my sister because she had a love affair. And I said to him, and you, although you're married to my mother, you're known all over town for womanizing. And you criticize a girl has a love affair? Yes, with one of my suppliers, the tanner, [? Hallenstein, ?] who afterwards went to Australia because he was also Jewish.

Anyway, my mother agreed, apparently, that I should be kicked out of the house. And many years later, when she came to Cape Town, I once asked her, when I was kicked out without a penny, out of the house, out of my job, what did you think? Why did you not tell my father, you can't do that to your own child, or my twin and my-- children?

She said, you were [GERMAN], which means, you were so naughty. Naughty.

INT: How did you manage to go to South Africa?

BH: I went to an attorney in my hometown. And I said, write a letter to my father. A, I want a testimonial that I was working for his firm, which he had inherited from my grandfather, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] so that I could show people in the country of destination [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] what kind of a job. I was capable of doing and that I had done it well.

I demand 100 pounds, because you had to deposit that with the South African government, unless you could-- had proof of employment prior to entry. And I want a third-class ticket to Cape Town. I went third class from Southampton to Cape Town.

INT: So you came back-- so you were in-- went to Holland.

BH: I went back to Germany.

INT: Then to Belgium, back to Germany again.

BH: Yes.

INT: And from Germany, how did you go to South Africa?

BH: Well, finally, when I emigrated to South Africa, I went via England-- the ship left from Southampton.

INT: Could you--

BH: Was a journey of two weeks.

INT: Could you describe this journey?

BH: Well, something interesting happened en route. We were in the high seas on the 9th of November, which is always observed in Britain as Armistice Day. Here, you have the celebration at the [? Cenotaph. ?] And the purser of the ship came to me-- there were about 19 refugees on board, Jewish refugees from Germany.

And he said, would you mind, sir, if we celebrated Armistice Day on board of the ship? I see a lot of German citizens on board. So I gave him a lesson in history. And I said, we will join you.

INT: When you arrived in Cape Town, is that the first South African port you docked at?

BH: Cape Town. Yes, we were received by a demonstration led by Dr. Verwoerd, who became later prime minister of South Africa. He was leading [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] a fascist organization called the Greyshirts with swastikas on their arms, protesting against Jews entering South Africa.

And I had three youngsters, who were much younger than I, 17, 18, also from my hometown, with me. I said, you see? Is our reception company-- a company, that they want us to feel at home. You-- you leave the swastika, and you're received by the swastika.

And we were so poor that we walked from the docks to the reception committee hall, was in the Zionist hall in-- in south Cape Town. Was in-- very hot. At this time, in the southern hemisphere, is the height of summer, in November, December, it's 35 40 degrees in-- in the sun, you know.

And we were received. And they asked us, have got money on you? Do you need financial assistance? Do you speak English?

So I said, yes, I do. So they said, oh, they just brought me in as an interpreter. Because the other immigrants couldn't speak a word of English. Mostly French, that's the only foreign language they knew.

And after two weeks, I found employment.

INT: What did you do?

BH: I was taken by the chairman of the refugee committee to a suburb called Woodstock and to an importer of chemicals, who hailed from Liepaja in Latvia, and came to South Africa in 1898. And wanted a greenhorn-- you know what I mean by greenhorn? Greenhorn is a new immigrant who is absolutely ignorant. But he wanted an educated one to do his books, to do his correspondence, et cetera.

And he offered me 30 shillings a week. And I told him, I'm sorry, I can't work for such a low wage. My boarding house costs me 2 pounds a week, so I'm 10 shillings short. Where should I take the money? The 100 pounds I-- you had to deposit with the government as a security that you didn't become a burden on the state. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

He said to me, beggars can't be choosers. So I said to him, I'm not a beggar. But it would appear to me that you can't afford to pay more, because your business is so small, and so not lucrative enough to pay more than 30 shillings. And I started walking out-- walking out.

So this chap who take taken me there, chairman of the refugee committee said, accept it first. More will follow. I said, and who's paying the difference between 30 shillings and 2 pounds? Said, I'm not prepared to work.

I speak English better than my boss, whose mother tongue was Yiddish. He had been there 35 years, and he expected me-- on-- on the way out, I must say, I studied Afrikaans, so I already could conduct English and Afrikaans correspondence.

So I started working there.

INT: For how much?

BH: Pardon?

INT: For how much?

BH: 2 pounds. And then I found out that I couldn't afford the bus fare to get there. I bought a bicycle on the installment plan. In those days, a bicycle cost 3 pounds, and you paid off half a crown, 2 shilling, sixpence a week.

So the boss said to me, you're a white man. You can't drive a bicycle in-- in Southern Africa. Only Kaffirs and coolies do that. So I said to him, but you don't pay white-- pay me a white man's wage. And I continued to come to work on a push bike-- never mind the color bar.

INT: Did you receive, while in South Africa in the '30s, did you receive news from Germany?

BH: I didn't hear from my parents for 22 years.

INT: How about your brother, or your sister? Did they write to you?

BH: No, my one sister followed me six months later. My twin was in Italy, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] then married a Czech Jewish boy, and followed him to Prague. I was in correspondence with her.

But when my sister died in '37, my parents were still in Hannover. They only left in '41. They never wrote to me. When I met them after 22 years, they never mentioned my sister, not a word.

INT: Did you hear from anybody else in the family from Germany?

BH: Yes, my cousins who had gone to Palestine, I was in correspondence with them, and friends.

INT: Did they write you about-- what did they write about the situation in Germany?

BH: No, there was press censorship, personal censorship. But we knew from-- from the newspapers.

INT: Well, that-- do you know, even though they didn't write to you, do you know what had happened to your parents after Kristallnacht?

BH: Well, my father was taken to Buchenwald concentration camp, which is near the city of Weimar, W-E-I-M-A-R, which is the culture city of Germany where Goethe and Schiller lived.

And he told me later, as they were unloaded from the railway train, there stood the Blackshirts with steel rods and leather whips. And they first thrashed these men. And I got the list of those-- mostly were attorneys, butchers, merchants.

So-- and then, they had to walk up to the concentration camp, which is on top of a mountain. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And there, they were beaten again. And they had to work in a stone quarry. But I believe after four months, they were let out again.

INT: We take a tape break here.

INT: This is tape four. I'm interviewing Bernhard Herzberg. You just told me about your father's time in Buchenwald and how he was let out. What happened after four months, when he was liberated or allowed to go?

BH: He went back to Hannover. And my brother, who was then training in Silesia, near Breslau [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] at an agricultural school, in preparation for emigration to Palestine, he said the Gestapo came into that establishment and took away all the Jewish teachers, and the pupils were left alone to fend for themselves. So he sneaked out of the camp and he took a train from Breslau to Hannover, found my mother at home alone.

Subsequent to his return from the concentration camp, he was arrested again. He was chairman of the old aged home. And Jews were not allowed to buy vegetables from Gentile grocers. But there were no Jewish grocers, so they had to secretly obtain foodstuffs from Gentile butchers and-- and grocers. For that he was arrested, for having contravened the regulations, and kept in a remand prison for three years.

INT: This was your father?

BH: Till 1941, until my mother obtained a visa to go to Cuba, to Havana, via Portugal through Vichy France, which occupied France.

INT: What happened to your younger brother? Did he go with his parents?

BH: My younger brother went to England and became a gardener on Lord Rothschild's estate at Southampton, based on his training in an agricultural college in Germany.

INT: What year was this that he left for England?

BH: In '39, a few months before the war. But unlike I, my father sent him on an airplane to London, where he was received by a friend of the family. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] After the war in France was over and the British troops evacuated through Dunkirk, they arrested all German nationals in Britain. And my brother, who was then 18, I think, was deported to Canada.

On the ship were Nazi sympathizers, German prisoners of war, Jewish national-- Jewish boys and-- not-- no-- no women, men. When they-- they landed in Canada, they were taken to an internment camp. And the kitchen was conquered by the Nazi prisoners. And they wouldn't let the Jews have any food. So there were [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] battles inside the internment camp until the British soldiers understood that one had to separate the victims from the Nazis.

But amongst the exiles were also German Communists, Gentile ones. And my brother came under their influence. And then, when I-- when I was in the army, I received a letter from Hans Herzberg, Prisoner of War Camp, Canada. So my-- the officer commanding the artillery and captain called me in. He said, who is this chap from whom you-- there was press censorship. I said, my brother.

But how can he be a German POW? So I explained to him that the British arrested the refugees and the Nazis together. So-- and I cabled to my former boss in Montreal, please-- please vouch for my brother's Jewish descent. And he was then sent back to Britain, became a farm laborer, joined the British army, became a staff sergeant in the British army as an interpreter.

INT: When did you get married?

BH: I or he?

INT: You.

BH: I met a girl called Ann [? Fisher, ?] who was a photographer. She arrived in '37, in 1937. We lived together, and when the war broke out I married her quickly so that she became a British subject in the Union, as it was then called. So-- because they would not allow her as an enemy alien to carry out her profession as a photographer.

INT: Had-- how was that possible? Were you no longer German?

BH: No, I became naturalized in March '39.

INT: And did you mention her name, of your wife, the lady you met?

BH: Yes, Ann [? Fisher. ?]

INT: Yes, sorry. And when did you volunteer-- did you volunteer for the army?

BH: Yes, on the day Paris fell, on my birthday, 21st June, 1940, we saw pictures of the Nazis unfurling the swastika flag under the Arc de Triomphe in Paris. And I volunteered for the infantry that night. And Ann was absolutely livid.

How can you leave me alone? I don't want to be a war widow. I've got no relations. I don't know anybody. And finally, she left me because I joined the army. But thereby hangs another story.

So I volunteered in '40. I had to do foot and rifle duty in my civvies, because there weren't enough uniforms. At that time, the South African army was in Abyssinia trying to defeat the Italians. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] So they sent me home. A year later, they came-- came to arrest me as a deserter.

So I said, how-- how can you say I deserted? You dismissed me. I'm not prepared to serve now, after this treatment. I gave up my job to join the army and then you dismissed me from the army. No, I had to join.

I took along my attorney. And he said, you better join, because they threatened they would intern me together with the Nazi POWs, who had been evacuated to South Africa unless I reported within 24 hours to the military authorities, based on my oath, which was a year old. So I had to swear another oath of loyalty to King George the VI. And I joined the Coast Artillery in Cape Town as a gunner.

INT: When did you meet your second wife?

BH: After my divorce in 1941.

INT: What's her name?

BH: Lilly Abrams, A-B-R-A-M-S.

INT: What sort of work was she doing when you met her?

BH: She was a typist in a trade union office for the distributive trade.

INT: And when did you get married?

BH: In January '44, a week before embarkation from Durban Harbor to Egypt.

INT: Could you describe your time in Egypt with the army?

BH: Well, first and foremost, it took 28 days for the convoy from Durban to Suez. It was a hellish journey. It was in a-- what you call a coolie ship-- 1,500 gunners in the hold-- in the cargo hold of the ship. There were five lavatories for 1,500 gunners, so they put up latrines on deck, sort of bucket arrangement, which overflowed.

And the crew-- the crew was [NON-ENGLISH], Indians. The ship came from the Far East. That's why it was called a coolie ship. It was used for transporting Chinese laborers to Hong Kong prior to the war.

Anyway, we landed in Egypt. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] we were sent to training camp at Helwan, H-E-L-W-A-N, in an area where no rain had fallen for 25 years. So the barracks had no roof, but the walls were infested with lice and bugs. It was very unpleasant. So I used to go out into the desert and dig a hole and sleep outside, because I didn't want to get bugs into my-- bugs into my uniform.

And then we were trained on coastal 12 pounder guns before going to Egypt. We were then trained on horse-drawn artillery from the First World War, which were obsolete. And we gunners had to pretend to be horses. We were given commands with our drag ropes and proceed to advance. And so when we got to Egypt, there were American Priest-- heavy caliber Priest guns. When we finally landed in Italy, we had British-- British 25 pounders. We had to be retrained again.

INT: Were you in any battles in Egypt?

BH: No, the-- the Battle of Alamein was over.

INT: So when did you go on to Italy? When was that?

BH: In March '44.

INT: What can you tell me about the time in Italy with the army?

BH: Well, we landed at Taranto, which is at the heel of Italy. And thereby also hangs a tale of antisemitism. The first night we landed we had no tents. They hadn't arrived from Egypt. We came by ship-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] very dangerous. We were attacked by a German U-boat. That is a submarine. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

So we had to dig holes for ourselves to sleep in in a-- in an olive orchard. And as we were digging, taking the stones out of the hard soil, I remembered from my school days that this part of Calabria was where the Spartacus legions fought the Romans. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And I said to another bombardier, I wonder whether by chance this is historical ground we've found, we will find old Roman coins. He said to me, how like a Jew to think of money. And I had a big shovel in my hand and I hit him over the head. And he collapsed. It was our first war casualty.

Nothing happened to me. He didn't report me. He was taken to hospital with a bashed in cheek. That was my first experience, the first night in-- and then we went to Casino,

Monte Casino was a famous mountain where the Germans were up in that monastery. But-- but we bypassed it and then went through the Abruzzi to a place called Capua, near Naples. And amongst the photographs I've got there is a photograph in Naples where by chance I met my Hamburg cousin who had emigrated to Palestine. And he was serving with the Jewish Brigade in-- in the British army.

INT: What was your reaction when you saw him?

BH: Overjoyed.

INT: Were you at any point asked to be an interpreter, a-- a German interpreter in the army?

BH: Yes, when we got to Egypt, one day I was called to the Psychological Warfare Bureau, which was run by a Major [? Berman, ?] a city councilor from Cape Town, who knew me and my antecedents. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I beg your pardon. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And he said, you're a German linguist? We'll promote you to captain and drop you by parachute behind the German lines in Italy to carry out acts of sabotage.

And I refused, because I said to him, anyone dropped behind enemy lines in civvies-- in civvies, not wearing a uniform is subject to immediate execution if caught. Besides being a German-born Jew, it will be a feast for the Nazis to put me against the wall, and I refused. So I remained in the artillery as a Bombardier until-- and sub-lieutenant until the end of the war.

INT: Were you made to wear dog tags as soldiers?

BH: What?

INT: Tags?

BH: Yes.

INT: What did yours say?

BH: H-E-B-- Hebrew.

INT: And what reason did they give to put that on your tag?

BH: The Catholics had C-A-T. The Dutch Reformed, a Calvinist, D-R. That was the rule. When I joined the army they said to me, what religion? I said, I'm an atheist, I've got no religion. He said, well, we'll make you a Dutch Reformed. I said, no, not Dutch Reformed. I said, I'm not a Calvinist. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I don't worship any particular deity.

What was your father? So I said, Jewish. So they gave me identity tag, which we had to wear around on the-- H-E-B. So I said, what will happen when the Germans capture me? Oh, the staff sergeant who gave me my uniform, he said that's your business, not mine.

INT: So you were in Italy, going through Italy, where did you-- where-- where were you on the day?

BH: Well, we from Capua, which was Spartacus city in history, which is not far from Naples, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] to the front, which was-- is Pistoia, which is north of Florence. And I remember passing through Rome at night. Rome was declared a free city. The Germans did not fight for it. You know, it-- they let the Allies in without a fight. Peeping through a crack in the cattle truck and I saw the lights of Rome.

And we landed at a place-- Castiglion [? de-- ?] [? de ?] Fiorentino, as it was called, near Arezzo. We were offloaded there. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] that was near the front. And the first night we slept in a little-- in this little village, which had a theater. At night the Americans' planes came over, thought the Germans were still in it and bombed us out. Anyway, they-- we survived. And the next day, we were taken to the front, which was at Pistoia.

But before reaching Pistoia, we had to go through Florence. Florence was liberated by the South African army. And all the bridges had been blown up, except the Ponte Vecchio, which is the famous old bridge with the-- all the shops there on the bridge. So we went over an American built Bailey bridge, a pontoon bridge. And sort of a little episode-- I stopped outside the famous cathedral in-- in Florence.

And I saw a woman approaching me, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] carrying a baby. [DRINKS] And she addressed me in Italian and explained that she was from France, married to an Italian Jew, who had joined the partisans, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] and hadn't eaten for five days. The baby was crying. And all she could say, [ITALIAN]-- where is the synagogue?

How did I know? I was first time in Florence. So as an Italian passerby, he told me what street-- [ITALIAN]. I'll never forget that name. So I said to get onto my truck and I deposited her, handed her over to a committee. The synagogue had been dynamited by the returning fascista, but there was a soup kitchen. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS] So that was Italy, but--

INT: Can I just take you back to Egypt again?

BH: Pardon?

INT: Can I just take you back to Egypt again? While there, did you manage at all to get to Palestine?

BH: No, I went to Palestine when I returned from Italy, after two years.

INT: Well, we'll get to that.

BH: I spent two years in Italy with the army. Only in [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] October of '45 did I get back to Egypt. We were a army of occupation.

INT: Can I then just--

BH: Hmm?

INT: --continue-- we'll get to that later then. Where were you on the day the war was over?

BH: In Gorgonzola, but there was no cheese there. They-- you know what they-- they used to say in Italy? [ITALIAN]-- the Germans have taken everything away, including the cheese.

INT: Could you describe that day of when the war was over?

BH: Yes, I was sitting on my truck, ammunition truck, and I had earphones on. And I heard the voice of the BBC, in a matter of fact voice, saying, yesterday General Wolff, commanding the German forces, surrendered unconditionally to the force-- our forces in Italy. And the war is over, if it were-- as if it was the most natural thing, in that very restrained BBC voice.

INT: So were you then the bearer of the news to all the others?

BH: No, well, we all knew it within minutes. Because the night before, we still had-- were on patrol. On the other side of the river was a German SS regiment. And we expected they would make a last stand, and it didn't materialize.

INT: What was the reaction of all of you when the war was over that day? What did you do?

BH: I think we went and brewed ourselves a cup of coffee, as far as I remember. [LAUGHS]

INT: When were you demobbed?

BH: In March '46. I got amoebic dysentery in Italy. And when I returned to Cape Town, before being demobbed, we had to go and be examined at the hospital. And they discovered I still had amoebic dysentery and the amoeba had gone into the liver. So I was in hospital till, I think, June '46.

INT: You say you went back via Egypt.

BH: Yes.

INT: And that's when you managed to get into Palestine?

BH: Yes.

INT: Could you tell me about that?

BH: Yes, it was very difficult. The British authorities were [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] most concerned about visitors to Palestine. And at-- in the citadel, which is the-- the old fortress in Cairo, there was a British high command. And I went in there, being in the Imperial forces-- South Africa then belong to the British Empire as it does again now-- and I applied to go on leave.

And the staff sergeant was very nasty to me. And I-- I said, listen, the war's over. There's no need to talk down to me. Either you allow me to go to Palestine or not, but don't give me long things-- long stories about Jewish boys carrying weapons into Palestine. I said, you didn't ask me what religion I belonged to. Well, I see-- I can see from your name. I said, my name is the name of a German town. It doesn't convey that to you.

Anyway, they allowed me to go to Palestine. At that time, you took the train from Cairo to Ismailia over the Suez Canal. And the railway line ended a Lod. Tel Aviv was not yet connected by rail. And from there, I went to Rehovot-- that's where the Weizmann Institute is. Took the bus to Gedera.

And the parents of that Harry Unna, who was photographed with me in Naples, lived there. He was a dentist in Hamburg. And there he lived, in the village. And he was so poor that he was walking barefoot and I had to sleep in the dentist chair because he didn't have a spare bed for me.

Anyway, from there I went into Tel Aviv, into Haifa, where the sister of my-- younger sister my mother lived, who was a high school teacher in Hamburg. And then I traveled up to [? Qoussair ?] on the Golan Heights, only then it was still occupied by Syria. It was on the border there, near Metula, if you know where that is. And I met old cousins and other people from Hamburg and Hannover, where I lived.

And then I went back-- no, what happened was the Irgun were busy. And the British high command in Jerusalem said all Imperial troops, including South Africans on leave in Palestine had to report for military duty in Palestine to fight Jewish terrorists. So I sneaked out of Jerusalem and went quickly back to the railhead.

And thereby, also hangs a funny story. This train came from Damascus to Cairo. It was only for officers. Now the South Africans had red tabs on their shoulders to indicate that we're volunteer. But in the British army, that was the son of a Brigadier. So as I reported to the road transportation officer at [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Lod railway station, he saluted me. And I had removed my two stripes, my sub-lieutenant stripes.

Where you off to, sir? We got a rail warrant. Yes, he said, but third class, an officer can't go third class. And so he put me into a compartment with a British major, who said to me, good evening sir, do you want the upper or the lower bunk, in-- on the trains. I said, it's immaterial to me. What regiment? So I give him the number, 422nd field artillery. Never heard of it. I said, no, South African army.

Really? In what battle were you? And then-- then, so I had to spin him a yarn and goodness knows what. Anyway, I got back to Cairo.

INT: I just want to ask you, when you entered Palestine for the first time, what was your reaction?

BH: I was on a military train and-- and we were all searched from top to bottom, looking for arms. Because soldiers coming from Cairo often carried revolver and ammunition. And I was still very annoyed about this, being searched again, as if I were an enemy alien, you know.

And I don't think I was particularly elated. But what struck me when I got to Gedera, I saw Jews sweeping streets with old-- with brooms. I thought, oh, at last the class bases of European Jewry has changed. They can sweep streets now.

