When-- we know when you were demobbed, but when did you get back to Cape Town?

BH: Christmas '45.

INT: Could you describe the arrival in Cape Town?

BH: Well, we arrived at Durban, which is the port on the east coast. And, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] on the frigate Transvaal,this was a frigate used for-- in coastal waters, carrying 150 gunners. And they said, oh, you, back to barracks. I said, I'm never going to another military barrack again.

I went to the first hotel on the beach front, and I-- I said, I went a room. The man said to me, who's going to pay? I said, General Smuts-- that was the commander in chief of the South African army. He said, oh, that's all right then.

And then, 10 gunners had followed me into my room, and they all had a bath. Because we were-- two weeks where we are this frigate, we had to sleep on deck. There were no bath facilities, except for officers.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the hotel manager came, he said, you rented a room, and you're paying for one room, and there are 10 gunners? I said, just leave us alone. If you don't want these chaps to have a bath after two weeks at sea without a bath, as if we're going to wreck your hotel, huh, just leave us alone.

We were very [INAUDIBLE] men. And you know, after five years in the army, we have had it, you know.

Anyway, then, finally, they hooked us-- our coach-- onto a goods train, without rations. Took five days to get to Cape Town through South Africa. So we came through a town called Bloemfontein, and we were very hungry. They had forgotten-- typical, the army fashion, something is always forgotten.

So I pulled the emergency brake. And the train driver said, who pulled the brake? I said, I did. Why? I said, we are hungry.

We all went into the station restaurants, and they ask us, who's paying for the meal? So I said, General Smuts.

So finally, when I got to Cape Town, I've-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I found Lily inside the station, but she hardly recognized me. Because after my amoebic dysentery, my weight was down to 50 kilos. I weigh 78 now.

So then, I went home. And as I mentioned before, I was again suffering from amoebic dysentery, so my demobilization was delayed. So the army rule was, you were not demobbed unless you could proof of employment.

So I went back to that chemical merchant, and I said, before the war, I earned 50 pounds a month, which was a living wage. How much are you going to pay me now?

Oh, he said, you have lost so much experience. I pay you 40 pounds. I said, how about inflation? I said, you bloody bastard, I never forgot-- I cursed him up and down.

I said, you made millions during the war? And we were up north fighting for you? And you want to reduce my salary? Uh-uh. I'd rather stay in the army. Anyway, he then reinstated me at 50 pounds a month.

INT: When did you discover what had happened to the rest of your family?

BH: On return to Cape Town.

INT: Did you manage to contact anybody?

BH: Well, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I wrote to my brother, who was in England. And my parents were in New York City. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] My twin sister had died. My other sister was in South Africa. And then my-- the family in Palestine, I knew. There were some in America, some in England, and in Rio de Janeiro.

INT: Had anybody survived in Germany?

BH: No.

INT: What had--

BH: Yeah-- no, in Italy, not in Germany.

INT: What were the names of the survivors, apart from your immediate family that you mentioned?

BH: Well, my father had one sister, I mentioned before. She committed suicide when the Gestapo came for her. Her son, Erich Cohen, who was an attorney, and escaped to Italy with his little son, [? Peter, ?] and was hidden by the monks in the monastery.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] they converted into Catholicism. And afterwards, when he was re-admitted as an attorney, and I met him for the first time in '55, on his desk stood the-- statue of the Virgin Mary, and next to it, a menorah.

I said, you-- he said, well, with a name like Cohen, I can't-- I cannot-- I cannot only worship the Virgin Mary, can I?

INT: What about the name of the person in Rio de Janeiro, who was that?

BH: [? Loewenhauer. ?] That was my mother's youngest sister.

INT: What was her first name?

BH: Trude, T-R-U-D-E. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] she went there with her husband, who came from Bavaria, and her two children. To get employment in Brazil at that time, that the children had to convert to Catholicism. Otherwise, they wouldn't get-- they were not allowed to work.

INT: So did they convert?

BH: Yes.

INT: What was the name of her husband and of her children?

BH: Cohen.

INT: The first names.

BH: Oh, [? Edith, ?] and the boy was called Fritz, and-- after his dead uncle who died as a German soldier in World War I.

INT: And their father's name was what?

BH: [? Julius. ?]

INT: When-- do you have children? Yes, we've seen your son. Do you have other children?

BH: Yeah, my daughter, Wendy, Wendy [? Ruth. ?]

INT: When was she born?

BH: 1946.

INT: And when was Paul born, your son?

BH: '53.

INT: What can you tell me about Wendy?

BH: Well, she was a very bright scholar. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] She went to the University of Cape Town and became a librarian. And she occupies a very high position in London now, as IMEC-- that's a company publishing-- they bought out MacMillan's [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] section of-- what is it-- medicinal magazines, or something like that. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] She's a computer expert.

INT: What about your son, Paul? What can you tell me about him?

BH: Paul is an actor and writer. But he had to serve in the South African army twice. You know, they-- every year, they had to go. And he was on the Angola border, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] adjacent to Namibia, the former German Southwest Africa.

And after his second tour of duty, he said to me, I'm not fighting for Mr. President Botha anymore, I'm not in favor of the apartheid regime. So I sent him to London.

Now, he had a South African passport and was not allowed to-- to work. But a friend of mine, a chemical exporter, put him on a [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] weekly basis, and paid him out of petty cash. And a year afterwards, Paul sent me a cable to Cape Town, I want to get back to university to do an a-- he studied drama at the University of Cape Town.

And he was also trained as a filmmaker at the Technical College in Pretoria. And [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] so I had to provide for him for a year to go back to the London School of Drama and Music, LAMDA.

INT: When was this? Which year, roughly?

BH: Early '70s, I think '75, it was. Yeah, 1975.

INT: So what happened after his drama course?

BH: Well, he found employment-- you know, actors find it very difficult to get permanent employment. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And he-- he's been a taxi driver, and all sorts of things to keep himself above water.

INT: Did anyone ever ask you about your-- not only your wartime experiences, but also your experiences in Nazi Germany?

BH: Yes, I lecture to various German schools, especially in the northern part in Schleswig-Holstein, there's a small port called Eckernforde, which was a submarine base during the war. It's opposite Denmark. And I made the acquaintance of a high school teacher who asked me to come to talk to her matriculants.

And then the other schools in Kiel, is also in the north, asked me. And finally, I did it at my own school in Hannover. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

And then, I talked about my life into a tape recorder, which my nephew who's a journalist in Berlin, held in front of me. And my memoirs are a translation of the German text of that tape recording.

INT: What was the-- what was the reaction of those German kids hearing you speak about the war and what had happened to you as a Jew?

BH: Very sympathetic. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] First and foremost, they had never seen what is called a live Jew, you know. Didn't exist-- doesn't exist in that part of Germany anymore. Now, there is an influx from the east again. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And it was very-- actually very moving. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] When I lectured in my own school in Hannover, that was reported in the press. And the heading was, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] early recognition of the Nazi evil saved Bernhard Herzberg from the Holocaust. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

And I've mentioned before, I found this whole atmosphere in Nazi Germany absolutely unbearable. You were insulted in the streets, you were boycotted, or whatever. You want to stay in a country where you're subjected to indignity? Or this business-- that article of Goebbels's came to reality. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] That was the extermination.

But I found out something interesting. The Germans occupied South West Africa. The Germans were late colonialists. And they conducted the war against the Herero tribe in Namibia.

And the general in charge of that force in 1904, issued a command on behalf of his imperial majesty, King Kaiser Wilhelm II, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] to kill every Herero man, woman, and child. They called it a [GERMAN]-- an order to exterminate every member of a tribe.

And that, the Nazis used in World War II, when they met at the Wannsee conference, and decided-- they issued a [GERMAN]. In Afrikaans, we say, [AFRIKAANS]. You know, it's the same Germanic root of the word. It was a-- so they had-- they had training in extermination.

INT: When did your parents get back to-- why did your parents come to South Africa? You mentioned they came to South Africa. Why?

BH: Yes, on the recommendation of that tanner, that Belgian tanner in Tirlemont. And he said-- they said to me, you must emigrate. But he said, England didn't allow-- afterwards, they allowed only children. But I was already 23.

INT: No, no, your parents-- they came to South Africa.

BH: Oh.

INT: Why did they come to South Africa?

BH: Because I asked them to. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] They were nearly 90, living four flights up in New York City, in a two-room flat without curtains, without a doormat, although my father received compensation in '55. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] And then, he received-- he worked in America, he was in receipt until he died at the age of 93, an American and a German pension.

INT: How would you say your war experiences, and your experiences in Germany, have influenced the way you brought up your children?

BH: You mean my work experience in South Africa, or where?

INT: No, your war experience.

BH: Oh, war experience. Well, I told them what's what. I mean, I told them what happened to me, that I had to fight against my native country, and why. And they're fully aware.

I mean, and Paul was a very strong Rugby player, and if anybody made an antisemitic remark at school, he beat them up. He-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] he's now 45, so he's not so [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] conscious of having to fight his way through.

INT: How have these experiences affected your own life, these war experiences?

BH: Well, originally, I was a pacifist, based on World War I. When I saw so many deaths-- I remember those notices in the newspapers, blackboarded. I mean, millions died.

So when I volunteered, it was actually against my principles. But there was more at stake. I thought, if Britain also gives up, and South Africa was a British colony, South Africa becomes Nazified.

[PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

I mean, my first wife, Ann [? Fisher, ?] didn't understand this. She said, no. What did Chamberlain do? You fight for Poland, who has a fascist general in charge, Pilsudski, he didn't want to fight for Czechoslovakia. Now he fights for fascist Poland. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

So some people couldn't understand. I said, no, we can't be neutral in this game.

INT: What work have you been doing for most of your life?

BH: Well, apart from the training I had in Canada and in the States and in Germany, I joined this firm of chemical merchants. In 1953, that's 20 years after I joined that firm, the old man died. And his son was a structural engineer, and not working in the firm, made me the managing director. And I'd been given 20% of the share capital. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

In 1958, after-- five years after the old man's death, after I had run that firm, the son sold that chemical company to a conglomerate of South African druggists. And I became the local managing director-- director.

After two years, they transferred me to Johannesburg as general manager of the nationwide organization. Was the largest chemical and pharmaceutical company in the country at that time. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

When I got there, I had a contract that, at that time, '58, 250 pounds a month was a lot of money in those days, and a free Mercedes motorcar, which I had to drive up to Johannesburg. When I reported to the office, I was told that my contract as general manager was not valid. I said, why? He said, because you are Jewish. So I said, what does it mean?

Yes, the director of that chemical conglomerate had to be a member of the most fashionable club in Johannesburg, called the Rand, R-A-N-D club. And all the buyers of the gold mines used to meet people of the chemical industry, and the deals were done. And he said, that club does not admit people of your origin.

The man who was in charge, the man called [? Jacobs, ?] was a baptized Jew-- I knew that he was baptized. And I really got livid. And I took the contract out of my pocket, and I tore it up into little bits. And I said, here's the contract, sell it to the Gentiles. And I walked out.

And they were after my blood. He said, you can't leave the service of this company. You're under contract. I said, I'm the general manager, I was told you will be his-- the general manager's assistant.

He knows nothing about chemistry. And you will advise him about the social side-- meeting the buyers of the gold mines-- you must leave to him, it's down at that club. And took the train back.

And at the age of 50, I started my own business with my life savings of 8,000 pounds, which, today, is the largest chemical company in South Africa, only I was a fool. When I sold out eight years later, I was paid out in South African currency, which is subject to a 90% devaluation, so I lost all my life savings again.

INT: What brought you to England?

BH: I was working for a company as manager in Cape Town, another chemical company, which I had joined. And they transferred me to London to open up buying offices. Because South Africa was subject to boycotts, and I found it very difficult to obtain chemical raw materials from certain countries. So we bought them here in England and exported them. England did not boycott South Africa.

INT: Had your daughter-- your son was already living here, then. When was this, you came to England? What year?

BH: It was [INAUDIBLE]-- I think you asked me, it was '75.

INT: You came here in 1975?

BH: Yeah, he came in '75.

INT: No, you.

BH: I came in '85.

INT: Yes, when did your daughter come to England?

BH: Also in 1977.

INT: Now, you-- you told me that you went back to Germany. When was the first time you went back to--

BH: '55.

INT: Did you go back to Hannover at that time?

BH: Yes.

INT: What was your reaction then?

BH: Oh, it's difficult to define. It was very sad. They're only cemeteries there now, only gravestones.

INT: Do you still think about the war?

BH: Do I--

INT: Do you still think about the war?

BH: About what?

INT: About your time in Germany, your time in the army?

BH: Occasionally, yes. Especially the painful scene when I stood in front of my parental home and was kicked out.

INT: What do you do nowadays?

BH: Well, as you know, I'm-- I'll be 90 in June next year, but I am at the London University doing an external BA German course, which is language and literature. I hope next year, or next May, I'll be finished.

And I've done a lot of writing. I've written a book, which was published in '86, which mostly short stories about my war experience and the return to Germany, called Wiedersehen,to see again, you know? This-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] and I did a poem which was broadcast over the South African radio at that time.

INT: Did you ever join a refugee organization?

BH: Jewish?

INT: Any refugee organization.

BH: Yeah, the Jewish Ex-Service League, yes.

INT: Have you got a message for future generations?

BH: In what regard? What-- what generation?

INT: Well, future-- what can-- what in your eyes could the future generation learn about what happened in the war and what happened to the Jews?

BH: Well, I wouldn't confine it to that. Because it's a larger picture than Jewry. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] I mean, if you take the Hitler period, there were 3,000 Gentile Germans in concentration camps, political opponents. I mean, so it was not only [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the Jewish aspect about which one should talk to one's children. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS]

The way I see it, the present economic situation shows that there's something wrong with the capitalistic world. That doesn't mean one must apply the Russian system, or the previous Russian system. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It's very difficult.

I mean, I look at my son, who's an actor, and very difficult to make a livelihood for-- not only for actors. 3 million unemployed again in this country, and it's growing. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

So the-- I really haven't got a message. I think you can't have a message just for-- for-- for Jews. I mean, those of us who survived by-- by chance.

INT: I'm not only speaking about Jews, I'm talking about all sorts of people.

BH: You've got to fight for justice and against poverty.

